        <div id="sprayer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Sprayer Types: The Choice That Comes Before Brand
          </h2>
          <p>
            Paint sprayers come in three families: airless, HVLP, and cup-fed handheld. They use fundamentally different technologies to atomize paint, and each has tasks it excels at and tasks where it's the wrong tool. Choosing the right technology matters more than choosing the right brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Airless Sprayers</h3>
          <p>
            Airless sprayers use a piston pump to pressurize paint to between 1,500 and 3,000 PSI and force it through a small nozzle, where it atomizes from the pressure release alone. They are the fastest sprayers, capable of putting down a gallon of paint in 5–10 minutes on broad surfaces. They handle unthinned latex, oil-based, and even some heavy-body coatings. The trade-offs are overspray (high pressure produces fine mist that drifts further than HVLP) and finish quality on fine work (atomization is slightly coarser than HVLP). Airless is the right choice for <strong>walls, ceilings, exteriors, fences, and decks</strong>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">HVLP (High Volume, Low Pressure)</h3>
          <p>
            HVLP sprayers use a turbine to blow a large volume of air at low pressure through the gun, atomizing paint by air shear rather than hydraulic pressure. The result is finer atomization, smaller overspray cloud, and significantly better transfer efficiency (more of the paint actually lands on the work). The trade-off is speed — HVLP moves much less paint per minute than airless — and viscosity tolerance — most HVLPs need paint thinned to flow through the gun properly. HVLP is the right choice for <strong>cabinets, furniture, trim, doors, and fine finishes</strong>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cup-Fed Handheld Airless</h3>
          <p>
            Handheld airless sprayers (like the Graco TrueCoat and Graco Ultra Cordless) put the airless pump in the gun itself, fed from a cup or container. They sacrifice some run time and cup capacity for true grab-and-go portability. The atomization is airless-quality — clean fan, good edge definition — but the form factor makes them ideal for single rooms, touch-ups, accent walls, and projects where setup time matters more than throughput. They are the right choice for <strong>punch-list work, accent walls, doors, and shutters</strong>.
          </p>

