        <div id="how-to-spray" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Spray Paint Like a Pro
          </h2>
          <p>
            Sprayer failures almost always trace back to technique rather than the tool. The same sprayer can produce a glass-smooth finish in trained hands and a runny, orange-peel mess in untrained ones. The technique is learnable in about an hour of practice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Strain Your Paint</h3>
          <p>
            Pour your paint through a paint strainer (cone-shaped nylon mesh, about $2 each at any paint store) before adding it to your sprayer. Even a brand-new can of paint contains skin fragments, dried bits, and other particles that cause clogs and finish defects. Strained paint runs cleanly through sprayer tips that would otherwise clog every few minutes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Test on Cardboard</h3>
          <p>
            Before you point the sprayer at the actual work, spray test passes on a large piece of cardboard or plywood. Adjust pressure (airless) or material flow (HVLP) until the pattern shows a clean, even fan with no tails (heavy stripes on the edges of the pattern, indicating insufficient pressure) and no fogging (excessive overspray, indicating too much pressure). This 2-minute calibration prevents hours of rework.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Hold the Gun Perpendicular</h3>
          <p>
            The single most common technique mistake is "fanning" the gun — pivoting your wrist through the pass so the gun arcs across the surface. This produces a heavy stripe in the middle (where the gun is closest) and light stripes on the edges (where it's farther away). Instead, move your entire arm parallel to the surface, keeping the gun the same distance throughout the pass. The gun should always be perpendicular to the surface, never angled.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Maintain Consistent Distance</h3>
          <p>
            Airless sprayers work best at 10–12 inches from the surface. HVLPs work best at 6–8 inches. Closer means heavier application and likely runs; farther means dry spray and orange-peel texture. Practice this distance until it's automatic — many users hold a stick or ruler in their non-dominant hand for the first few projects as a distance reference.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Overlap Passes by 50%</h3>
          <p>
            Each spray pass should overlap the previous pass by 50%. The spray pattern is heaviest in the center and lightest at the edges; 50% overlap puts each section of wall under the heavy center of two passes, producing even coverage. Less overlap leaves stripes; more overlap wastes paint and risks runs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Trigger Before and After the Edge</h3>
          <p>
            Start pulling the trigger before the gun reaches the work edge and release it after the gun passes the opposite edge. Triggering directly at the edge creates a heavy buildup where the gun was stationary; triggering past the edge keeps the gun moving when paint is flowing, producing clean starts and stops.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Prep & Masking: Where Most Sprayer Projects Are Won or Lost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spraying produces overspray. Even the best HVLPs put fine paint mist into the air; airless sprayers can drift paint mist 10–15 feet from the work surface in still air. The single biggest difference between professional and amateur spray jobs is prep time, and the ratio of prep to spray is typically 3:1 — three hours of masking and protecting for every hour of actual spraying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Plastic Sheeting Everywhere</h3>
          <p>
            For interior work, cover everything within 15 feet of the work area with plastic sheeting taped at the seams. Floors, furniture, light fixtures, switch plates, baseboards, and trim all need protection. Painter's plastic (0.7 mil or thicker) drapes well over furniture. Floor protection should be cardboard or rosin paper rather than thin plastic, which tears under foot traffic and creates slip hazards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Quality Tape Matters</h3>
          <p>
            Cheap masking tape lets paint bleed underneath and leaves residue when removed. Use Frog Tape or 3M Blue Painter's Tape on edges where you need crisp lines. Apply tape carefully with a putty knife to seal the edge, and remove it before the paint fully cures (24 hours maximum) for the cleanest line.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Ventilation and Respiration</h3>
          <p>
            Spray atomization creates respirable paint particles. Even with the most diligent masking, you will breathe paint mist without a respirator. A half-mask respirator with organic vapor (OV) and P100 particulate cartridges is the minimum protection for interior spray work. For exteriors with good ventilation, a P100 dust mask is acceptable for water-based paints but not oil-based.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Prep Mistakes That Ruin Spray Jobs</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Insufficient masking — overspray drifts further than you expect</li>
              <li>• Skipping the paint strainer — particles clog tips and ruin finish</li>
              <li>• Spraying without a respirator — long-term health consequence, not just discomfort</li>
              <li>• Spraying in windy conditions outdoors — pattern distortion and overspray drift</li>
              <li>• Not test-spraying on cardboard before the actual work</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Climate and Conditions: When to Spray, When to Wait
          </h2>
          <p>
            Spray painting is more weather-sensitive than brush or roller application because the atomized paint droplets are exposed to ambient conditions during the brief moment between leaving the gun and landing on the surface. Temperature, humidity, and wind all affect the final result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Temperature</h3>
          <p>
            Most latex paints spray best between 50°F and 85°F. Below 50°F, the paint becomes too viscous to atomize properly and the film fails to coalesce. Above 85°F, the paint dries on the way from the gun to the surface, producing dry spray, orange-peel texture, and reduced adhesion. Check the paint can — most manufacturers print application temperature ranges and they are not suggestions. For exterior work, also consider surface temperature: a south-facing wall in summer can be 110°F even when the air is 80°F, which dries spray on contact.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Humidity</h3>
          <p>
            High humidity (above 80%) slows drying significantly and can cause water-based paints to "blush" — develop a hazy, milky appearance as moisture is trapped in the curing film. Low humidity (below 30%) accelerates drying to the point that spray dries before it lands. Aim for 40–60% relative humidity for best results. If you're spraying outside during the summer, morning hours typically have better humidity than afternoon, plus cooler temperatures and less wind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Wind</h3>
          <p>
            For exterior spraying, wind above 5 mph causes spray pattern distortion and overspray drift that can deposit fine paint mist on neighbor's cars, decks, and windows. Calm mornings are typically the best window for exterior spray work. If wind picks up mid-project, stop spraying and brush or roll the remaining work — pushing through windy conditions guarantees rework.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            What to Spray (and What Not to)
          </h2>
          <p>
            Not every paint job benefits from spraying. The decision depends on the surface area, surface complexity, and how much masking the surroundings require.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Spray these:</strong> exterior siding, fences, decks, full interior repaints (rooms empty of furniture), kitchen cabinets removed from boxes, doors removed from hinges, shutters, picket fences, sheds, garage doors, large pieces of furniture being refinished. The common thread is large, accessible surface area where masking the surroundings is feasible.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Don't spray these:</strong> single-wall touch-ups in occupied rooms, small trim repairs, ceiling-only jobs in furnished rooms, anything in windy outdoor conditions, ornate furniture pieces where overspray will collect in crevices. The masking time exceeds the paint time, and a brush or small roller is more efficient.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Spray with caution:</strong> furniture in place (mask aggressively or move), trim work with finished walls behind it (use cardboard shields, mask carefully), ceilings in occupied rooms (mask the floor and all furniture). These are doable but require setup time that often exceeds the spray time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Real-World Testing: How We Evaluated Each Sprayer
          </h2>
          <p>
            Every sprayer in this list was run through the same five-project gauntlet over six months of testing. We made a point of using each tool the way an actual homeowner would, not in a controlled lab setting that hides real-world weaknesses. The five test projects were chosen to expose different aspects of sprayer performance: a 12-by-12 foot bedroom with two coats of interior latex over primed drywall (tests speed and finish quality on flat surfaces), a kitchen cabinet refinish involving 18 doors, 22 drawer fronts, and the boxes still in place (tests fine-finish capability and cup capacity), a 200-foot wooden privacy fence with two coats of oil-based stain (tests run time, fuel consumption, and durability of the pump), a 2,400 square-foot exterior repaint of a two-story house (tests sustained operation and consistent atomization over hours of use), and a small dresser refinished in chalk paint with hand-rubbed wax finish (tests low-flow precision work on intricate surfaces).
          </p>
          <p>
            On each project, three different testers rotated through the sprayers so individual technique wouldn't bias the results. We tracked actual measured outcomes: square feet covered per gallon, time required for a full project including setup and cleanup, weight of overspray captured on drop cloths, finish defects requiring touch-up, and any maintenance issues encountered. The Graco Magnum X7 and Wagner Control Pro 130 both completed all five projects without significant problems. The HVLP units excelled on the cabinet and dresser projects but were noticeably slow on the fence and exterior. The handheld units (TrueCoat 360 DS and Graco Ultra Cordless) shined on the bedroom and were impractically slow on the larger projects, as expected for their form factor.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cleanup time was tracked separately because it influences how often a sprayer actually gets used. A sprayer that takes 30 minutes to clean tends to get put away dirty 'just this once' and then become unusable until the next deep cleaning session. The Graco Magnum X7's PowerFlush garden-hose system cut cleanup to about 5 minutes, the Wagner FLEXiO 5000 cleaned in about 8 minutes with included brushes, and the cup-fed handhelds required 10-15 minutes for thorough cleaning. Budget HVLPs typically took 15-20 minutes for proper cleaning, which directly correlated with how often we reached for them in the months after the initial test.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Maintenance: Making Your Sprayer Last
          </h2>
          <p>
            Paint sprayers are precision tools that suffer dramatically from neglect. The single most important maintenance practice is thorough cleaning immediately after every use — paint that dries inside the pump, in the hose, or in the gun assembly is extremely difficult to remove and progressively damages the internal seals and check valves. Set aside time at the end of every spray session for proper cleanup, even if you're tired and the cleanup feels tedious. The 15 minutes of cleanup now prevents the 2-hour deep-cleaning required when paint has cured inside the pump.
          </p>
          <p>
            For airless sprayers, run pump preserver through the system if you're storing it for more than two weeks between uses. Pump preserver is a mineral-spirit-based solution that displaces water from internal pump parts and prevents corrosion. Without it, the steel piston, valves, and cylinder can develop minor rust spots that score the precision-fit surfaces and cause future leakage. A bottle of pump preserver costs about $15 and extends pump life by years.
          </p>
          <p>
            HVLP turbines need their air filters cleaned regularly — a clogged filter reduces airflow, which reduces atomization quality and produces orange-peel finish on what should be smooth surfaces. Check the filter every 10 hours of use, clean with compressed air or a soft brush, and replace annually for hobby use or quarterly for heavy use. Most HVLP problems traced to 'the gun isn't atomizing right' are actually clogged turbine filters.
          </p>
          <p>
            Replace tips and nozzles when they show wear. Spray tips are wear items — they're designed to be replaced every 15-20 gallons of use, and a worn tip produces an asymmetric spray pattern, fingering on the edges, and inconsistent material flow. The cost is about $20-40 per tip, and the performance difference between a new tip and a worn one is dramatic. Keep at least one spare tip of your most-used size on hand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Airless or HVLP — which should I buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">If your first project is a fence, exterior, or full room repaint, buy airless (Graco Magnum X7 or Wagner Control Pro 130). If your first project is kitchen cabinets, furniture, or trim, buy HVLP (Wagner FLEXiO 5000 or HomeRight Finish Max). Most serious DIYers eventually own one of each because the technologies excel at fundamentally different tasks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to thin paint for a sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Most modern airless sprayers (Graco Magnum, Wagner Control Pro, Graco Ultra) spray unthinned latex straight from the can. Most consumer HVLPs require 10–20% water thinning for latex. Stains, lacquers, and varnishes spray at full strength through both types. Always check the manufacturer's viscosity recommendations before thinning.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does cleanup take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A well-designed airless with a flush adapter (like the Graco Magnum X7) cleans in 5–10 minutes. A cup-fed HVLP cleans in 10–15 minutes. Cheaper sprayers can take 20–30 minutes to clean properly. Cleanup time matters more than most buyers realize — sprayers that are tedious to clean tend to get used less often, regardless of how well they spray.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are paint sprayers worth it for one project?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For a single small project (one room, one fence panel), renting a sprayer is more economical. For a fence repaint, exterior repaint, or kitchen cabinet refinish, buying a $200–$500 sprayer pays for itself by the end of the project in time savings and finish quality. The Graco Magnum X7 typically saves 60–70% of project time versus brush/roller on large surfaces.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I spray over my old paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, if the old paint is well-adhered and properly prepared. Scuff-sand glossy old paint with 220-grit before spraying for adhesion. If the old paint is peeling, flaking, or chalking, you need to address those issues first — spraying over failing paint just spreads the failure under a new layer. Always prime over bare wood, drywall repairs, and stained areas.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

