        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Paint Sprayer Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sprayer moves paint faster than any other tool and makes mistakes faster too. Most poor results trace back to setup rather than technique.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Thinning or Straining the Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most sprayers have a viscosity limit, and paint at the wrong consistency spits, spatters, and clogs the tip mid-pass. Straining removes the skin and dried flecks that cause most tip blockages. Both steps take five minutes and prevent the majority of spray-day frustrations.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Wrong Tip Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tip size controls both the fan width and how much material comes out, and a tip meant for stain will not pass wall paint while a wide tip floods trim. Match the tip to the coating and the workpiece — it matters more to the finish than the sprayer model does.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Arcing the Wrist Instead of Moving the Arm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pivoting at the wrist changes the distance to the surface across the pass, so the middle goes on heavy and the ends go on thin. Keep the gun perpendicular and a constant distance away, and move the whole arm parallel to the surface. Start the pass before the edge and release after it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Under-Masking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Overspray travels much further than people expect, particularly with airless units, and it settles as a fine grit on cars, windows, and neighbouring surfaces. Mask further out than feels necessary and check the wind before spraying outdoors. Cleaning overspray takes far longer than masking would have.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">How do I clean a paint sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Water-based paints: flush with warm water until it runs clear, then run mineral spirits or pump preserver through to remove any residue. Oil-based paints: flush with mineral spirits until clear. For airless sprayers with garden-hose adapters, attach the hose and run water through until the discharge is clear (about 2 minutes). Disassemble the gun and clean the filter and tip with a soft brush.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">How much overspray should I expect from a paint sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Airless sprayers produce more overspray than HVLP. Plan to mask everything within 10–15 feet of the work area with plastic sheeting or drop cloths. HVLP overspray is more contained but still requires masking. Always wear a respirator rated for paint vapors and work in a ventilated space.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Can I spray latex paint with an HVLP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Yes, but most consumer HVLPs require thinning latex 10–20% with water to flow properly through the gun. Higher-powered HVLPs like the Wagner FLEXiO 5000 can spray unthinned latex with the X-Boost nozzle dialed up. If you'll spray a lot of unthinned latex, an airless is the better tool.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

